(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("leadership",{"leadership/star-stories-and-behavioral-interviews":{"html":"\u003cp>Behavioral interviews ask what you did, not what you know. Interviewers want evidence of judgement under pressure, and a small set of well prepared stories covers most questions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What interviewers look for\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The STAR structure and its common failure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Building a story bank\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding numbers, decisions and trade-offs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handling follow up questions and failure stories\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-they-look-for\">What they look for\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Interviewers test for ownership, judgement, collaboration, learning from mistakes and impact. They listen for what you personally decided and did. A team result with no clear role for you tells them little.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"star\">STAR\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Situation and Task set the scene in two or three sentences. Action is the longest part: what you chose, why and how. Result states the outcome with a number and what you learned. The common failure is to spend most of the time on the situation and then rush the action.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-story-bank\">The story bank\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Prepare six to eight stories that each fit several questions: a hard outage, a disagreement, a project you led, a mistake, a time you influenced without authority, a time you mentored and a time you made a trade-off with incomplete data. Write each as five bullet points, not a script.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"numbers-and-trade-offs\">Numbers and trade-offs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Say how many users were affected, how long it lasted, what it cost and what improved. Explain the options you rejected and why. Senior interviewers value the reasoning more than the success.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"follow-ups-and-failures\">Follow ups and failures\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Expect questions such as what would you do differently and what did others think. Choose a real failure with a real lesson, and describe the change you made afterwards. Avoid disguised bragging such as caring too much.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"q&gt;s;s&gt;a;a&gt;f\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">STAR stories and behavioral interviews\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"q\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Question\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"s\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Story bank\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"a\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">STAR answer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"f\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Follow ups\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Question to Story bank; 2, Story bank to STAR answer; 3, STAR answer to Follow ups.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"q\">\u003cdt>Question \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Asks for an example.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Vague.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"s\">\u003cdt>Story bank \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Matching story.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too generic.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"a\">\u003cdt>STAR answer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Action focused.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Long setup.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"f\">\u003cdt>Follow ups \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Probe your role.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> We not I.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-answer-that-said-we-thirty-times\">Incident: The answer that said we thirty times\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A candidate described a large migration and could not say what they personally did.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -o -i -w &quot;we&quot; answer.txt | wc -l\ngrep -o -i -w &quot;I&quot; answer.txt | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -o -i -w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;we&quot;\u003c/span> answer.txt | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">31\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -o -i -w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;I&quot;\u003c/span> answer.txt | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The interviewer asked what the candidate decided and got a vague reply. The candidate rewrote the story with decisions in the first person: the cutover plan, the rollback criteria and the call to delay a week. The next answer was clearer and led to an offer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Describing the team and not your own actions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Long background and a short action\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No numbers in the result\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stories that never show a mistake\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Memorised scripts that fall apart under follow ups\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-they-look-for","text":"What they look for"},{"id":"star","text":"STAR"},{"id":"the-story-bank","text":"The story bank"},{"id":"numbers-and-trade-offs","text":"Numbers and trade-offs"},{"id":"follow-ups-and-failures","text":"Follow ups and failures"},{"id":"incident-the-answer-that-said-we-thirty-times","text":"Incident: The answer that said we thirty times"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Atlassian team playbook","u":"https://www.atlassian.com/team-playbook"},{"t":"Postmortem culture","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/"},{"t":"Code review developer guide","u":"https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/"}],"text":"Behavioral interviews ask what you did, not what you know. Interviewers want evidence of judgement under pressure, and a small set of well prepared stories covers most questions. What you will learn What interviewers look for The STAR structure and its common failure Building a story bank Adding numbers, decisions and trade-offs Handling follow up questions and failure stories What they look for Interviewers test for ownership, judgement, collaboration, learning from mistakes and impact. They listen for what you personally decided and did. A team result with no clear role for you tells them little. STAR Situation and Task set the scene in two or three sentences. Action is the longest part: what you chose, why and how. Result states the outcome with a number and what you learned. The common failure is to spend most of the time on the situation and then rush the action. The story bank Prepare six to eight stories that each fit several questions: a hard outage, a disagreement, a project you led, a mistake, a time you influenced without authority, a time you mentored and a time you made a trade-off with incomplete data. Write each as five bullet points, not a script. Numbers and trade-offs Say how many users were affected, how long it lasted, what it cost and what improved. Explain the options you rejected and why. Senior interviewers value the reasoning more than the success. Follow ups and failures Expect questions such as what would you do differently and what did others think. Choose a real failure with a real lesson, and describe the change you made afterwards. Avoid disguised bragging such as caring too much. STAR stories and behavioral interviews Flow in order: 1, Question to Story bank; 2, Story bank to STAR answer; 3, STAR answer to Follow ups. Outside party Data store Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Question Outside party What it does: Asks for an example. If it fails or is weak: Vague. Story bank Data store What it does: Matching story. If it fails or is weak: Too generic. STAR answer Step What it does: Action focused. If it fails or is weak: Long setup. Follow ups Outside party What it does: Probe your role. If it fails or is weak: We not I. Incident: The answer that said we thirty times Incident A candidate described a large migration and could not say what they personally did. $ grep -o -i -w \"we\" answer.txt | wc -l 31 $ grep -o -i -w \"I\" answer.txt | wc -l 2 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The interviewer asked what the candidate decided and got a vague reply. The candidate rewrote the story with decisions in the first person: the cutover plan, the rollback criteria and the call to delay a week. The next answer was clearer and led to an offer. Common mistakes Describing the team and not your own actions Long background and a short action No numbers in the result Stories that never show a mistake Memorised scripts that fall apart under follow ups"},"leadership/communicating-technical-decisions-and-design-reviews":{"html":"\u003cp>A decision nobody can find or understand will be remade every year. Clear short writing and well run reviews save more time than any tool.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The decision record: context, options, decision, consequences\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing a design document that gets read\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running a design review\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explaining to non technical stakeholders\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disagreeing and committing\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"decision-records\">Decision records\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An architecture decision record is one page: the context and constraints, the options considered, the decision, and the consequences including costs. Store it next to the code. When a new person asks why, you can answer with a link.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"design-documents\">Design documents\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with the problem and goals, then non goals, then the proposal, alternatives and risks. Put a summary first, because many readers stop after a paragraph. Include a diagram and the rollout and rollback plan. Ask for specific feedback, such as risks you may have missed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"running-a-review\">Running a review\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Send the document in advance and ask people to comment in writing. Use the meeting to resolve open questions, not to read the document aloud. Separate blocking concerns from preferences, record decisions and name an owner for each action.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"non-technical-readers\">Non technical readers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Lead with the business effect: cost, risk, time and customer impact. Use one analogy at most, give a range with the assumption behind it and state what you need from them, such as approval or budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"disagree-and-commit\">Disagree and commit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When the decision is made, support it even if you argued against it. Write down your concern and the signal that would show the decision was wrong, so the team can revisit it with evidence instead of feelings.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"ctx&gt;opt;opt&gt;dec;dec&gt;rev\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Communicating technical decisions and design reviews\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ctx\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Context and goals\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"opt\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Options\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dec\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Decision record\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rev\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Context and goals to Options; 2, Options to Decision record; 3, Decision record to Review.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ctx\">\u003cdt>Context and goals \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Problem and limits.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Solution first.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"opt\">\u003cdt>Options \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Real alternatives.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Straw men.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dec\">\u003cdt>Decision record \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Chosen path and costs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not stored.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rev\">\u003cdt>Review \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Written feedback.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Meeting only.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-decision-made-three-times\">Incident: A decision made three times\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two years after a database choice, a new team proposed the same change and the debate restarted.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git log --oneline --diff-filter=A -- docs/adr | wc -l\ngrep -ril &quot;why we chose&quot; docs | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline --diff-filter=A -- docs/adr | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -ril \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;why we chose&quot;\u003c/span> docs | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>No decision record existed, so the context was lost when the authors moved on. The team introduced a short record template, wrote the missing record from memory and linked it from the repository README so the next debate started from facts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Starting with the solution and no problem statement\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Straw man alternatives\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reviews that read the document aloud\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Technical detail for an executive reader\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No record of the decision\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"decision-records","text":"Decision records"},{"id":"design-documents","text":"Design documents"},{"id":"running-a-review","text":"Running a review"},{"id":"non-technical-readers","text":"Non technical readers"},{"id":"disagree-and-commit","text":"Disagree and commit"},{"id":"incident-a-decision-made-three-times","text":"Incident: A decision made three times"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Code review developer guide","u":"https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/"},{"t":"Atlassian team playbook","u":"https://www.atlassian.com/team-playbook"},{"t":"Postmortem culture","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/"}],"text":"A decision nobody can find or understand will be remade every year. Clear short writing and well run reviews save more time than any tool. What you will learn The decision record: context, options, decision, consequences Writing a design document that gets read Running a design review Explaining to non technical stakeholders Disagreeing and committing Decision records An architecture decision record is one page: the context and constraints, the options considered, the decision, and the consequences including costs. Store it next to the code. When a new person asks why, you can answer with a link. Design documents Start with the problem and goals, then non goals, then the proposal, alternatives and risks. Put a summary first, because many readers stop after a paragraph. Include a diagram and the rollout and rollback plan. Ask for specific feedback, such as risks you may have missed. Running a review Send the document in advance and ask people to comment in writing. Use the meeting to resolve open questions, not to read the document aloud. Separate blocking concerns from preferences, record decisions and name an owner for each action. Non technical readers Lead with the business effect: cost, risk, time and customer impact. Use one analogy at most, give a range with the assumption behind it and state what you need from them, such as approval or budget. Disagree and commit When the decision is made, support it even if you argued against it. Write down your concern and the signal that would show the decision was wrong, so the team can revisit it with evidence instead of feelings. Communicating technical decisions and design reviews Flow in order: 1, Context and goals to Options; 2, Options to Decision record; 3, Decision record to Review. Outside party Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Context and goals Outside party What it does: Problem and limits. If it fails or is weak: Solution first. Options Step What it does: Real alternatives. If it fails or is weak: Straw men. Decision record Data store What it does: Chosen path and costs. If it fails or is weak: Not stored. Review Step What it does: Written feedback. If it fails or is weak: Meeting only. Incident: A decision made three times Incident Two years after a database choice, a new team proposed the same change and the debate restarted. $ git log --oneline --diff-filter=A -- docs/adr | wc -l 0 $ grep -ril \"why we chose\" docs | wc -l 0 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) No decision record existed, so the context was lost when the authors moved on. The team introduced a short record template, wrote the missing record from memory and linked it from the repository README so the next debate started from facts. Common mistakes Starting with the solution and no problem statement Straw man alternatives Reviews that read the document aloud Technical detail for an executive reader No record of the decision"},"leadership/influence-without-authority":{"html":"\u003cp>Platform and SRE engineers ask other teams to change things they do not control. Influence comes from trust, shared goals and lowering the cost of saying yes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Start with their goals\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trust through small kept promises\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Evidence and a clear ask\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Making the right thing the easy thing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Escalating without damaging relationships\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-with-their-goals\">Start with their goals\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A team that is behind on a deadline will not welcome a request to upgrade a library. Learn their priorities and show how your request helps them, for example fewer pages at night or a faster release. If it does not help them, say so and agree on timing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"trust\">Trust\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Do small things you said you would do, answer promptly and share credit. Trust is built over months and lost in one broken promise. Be the person who brings a draft pull request, not only a request.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"evidence-and-ask\">Evidence and ask\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use data from their own systems: error rates, cost, incident counts. Make a specific ask with an owner and a date, and state the cost of doing nothing. A vague complaint produces a vague reply.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"easy-defaults\">Easy defaults\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Provide templates, libraries, pipelines and automation so the secure or reliable path takes less effort than any other. Offer to do the first migration with them. Adoption follows convenience more than mandates.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"escalation\">Escalation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Escalate only after a direct conversation, and tell the other person first. Present the problem and options to the shared manager, not the person. Accept that sometimes the answer is not now and plan for it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"goal&gt;trust;trust&gt;ask;ask&gt;easy\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Influence without authority\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"goal\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Their goals\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"trust\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Trust\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ask\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Clear ask with data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"easy\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Easy default\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Their goals to Trust; 2, Trust to Clear ask with data; 3, Clear ask with data to Easy default.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"goal\">\u003cdt>Their goals \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Learn priorities.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring them.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"trust\">\u003cdt>Trust \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Kept promises.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Broken promise.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ask\">\u003cdt>Clear ask with data \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Owner and date.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Vague complaint.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"easy\">\u003cdt>Easy default \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Template or tool.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Mandate only.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-mandate-that-nobody-followed\">Incident: The mandate that nobody followed\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A platform team announced that all services must adopt a new logging library by quarter end.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -rl &quot;legacy-logger&quot; services | wc -l\ngrep -rl &quot;new-logger&quot; services | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -rl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;legacy-logger&quot;\u003c/span> services | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">87\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -rl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;new-logger&quot;\u003c/span> services | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Teams had no time and no help, so most ignored the mandate. The platform team then offered pull requests that did the migration for ten services, a codemod for the rest and a dashboard that showed adoption by team. Within two months eighty percent had moved.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Mandates with no help\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Complaining without data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Escalating before talking\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Breaking small promises\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the other team priorities\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-with-their-goals","text":"Start with their goals"},{"id":"trust","text":"Trust"},{"id":"evidence-and-ask","text":"Evidence and ask"},{"id":"easy-defaults","text":"Easy defaults"},{"id":"escalation","text":"Escalation"},{"id":"incident-the-mandate-that-nobody-followed","text":"Incident: The mandate that nobody followed"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Atlassian team playbook","u":"https://www.atlassian.com/team-playbook"},{"t":"Communication and collaboration in SRE","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/communication-and-collaboration/"},{"t":"Postmortem culture","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/"}],"text":"Platform and SRE engineers ask other teams to change things they do not control. Influence comes from trust, shared goals and lowering the cost of saying yes. What you will learn Start with their goals Trust through small kept promises Evidence and a clear ask Making the right thing the easy thing Escalating without damaging relationships Start with their goals A team that is behind on a deadline will not welcome a request to upgrade a library. Learn their priorities and show how your request helps them, for example fewer pages at night or a faster release. If it does not help them, say so and agree on timing. Trust Do small things you said you would do, answer promptly and share credit. Trust is built over months and lost in one broken promise. Be the person who brings a draft pull request, not only a request. Evidence and ask Use data from their own systems: error rates, cost, incident counts. Make a specific ask with an owner and a date, and state the cost of doing nothing. A vague complaint produces a vague reply. Easy defaults Provide templates, libraries, pipelines and automation so the secure or reliable path takes less effort than any other. Offer to do the first migration with them. Adoption follows convenience more than mandates. Escalation Escalate only after a direct conversation, and tell the other person first. Present the problem and options to the shared manager, not the person. Accept that sometimes the answer is not now and plan for it. Influence without authority Flow in order: 1, Their goals to Trust; 2, Trust to Clear ask with data; 3, Clear ask with data to Easy default. Outside party Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Their goals Outside party What it does: Learn priorities. If it fails or is weak: Ignoring them. Trust Step What it does: Kept promises. If it fails or is weak: Broken promise. Clear ask with data Step What it does: Owner and date. If it fails or is weak: Vague complaint. Easy default Service What it does: Template or tool. If it fails or is weak: Mandate only. Incident: The mandate that nobody followed Incident A platform team announced that all services must adopt a new logging library by quarter end. $ grep -rl \"legacy-logger\" services | wc -l 87 $ grep -rl \"new-logger\" services | wc -l 4 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Teams had no time and no help, so most ignored the mandate. The platform team then offered pull requests that did the migration for ten services, a codemod for the rest and a dashboard that showed adoption by team. Within two months eighty percent had moved. Common mistakes Mandates with no help Complaining without data Escalating before talking Breaking small promises Ignoring the other team priorities"},"leadership/mentoring-and-giving-feedback":{"html":"\u003cp>Senior engineers multiply their impact by growing others. That needs skills of its own: asking good questions, giving clear feedback and delegating with the right amount of support.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What mentoring is and is not\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The feedback structure: observation, impact, request\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reviewing code as teaching\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Receiving feedback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Delegating for growth\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mentoring\">Mentoring\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A mentor helps someone reach their goals by asking questions, sharing experience and opening doors. It is not doing the work for them. Agree on goals, a regular time and what the mentee wants to practise. Let them find answers first and step in when they are stuck.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"feedback\">Feedback\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>State what you observed, the effect it had and what you suggest. For example: in the review you interrupted twice, so the quieter engineers stopped giving input, so please wait until others finish. Give it soon after the event, in private for criticism and in public for praise, and focus on behaviour, not character.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"code-review-as-teaching\">Code review as teaching\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Explain the reason behind a comment and link to a guideline. Mark preferences as optional and separate them from blocking issues. Praise good solutions specifically, because people repeat what is noticed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"receiving-feedback\">Receiving feedback\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Listen to the end, ask for an example and thank the person even if you disagree. Decide later what to change. Ask peers and your manager for feedback regularly instead of waiting for a review cycle.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"delegating\">Delegating\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Match the task to the person: stretching but possible. Explain the outcome you want and the limits, not each step. Agree when you will check in and let them make small mistakes. Credit them when it goes well.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"goal&gt;obs;obs&gt;task;task&gt;rev\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Mentoring and giving feedback\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"goal\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Goals\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"obs\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Observation and impact\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"task\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Stretch task\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rev\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Check in\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Goals to Observation and impact; 2, Observation and impact to Stretch task; 3, Stretch task to Check in.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"goal\">\u003cdt>Goals \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Agree what to practise.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No goal.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"obs\">\u003cdt>Observation and impact \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Specific feedback.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Vague.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"task\">\u003cdt>Stretch task \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Right size.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too big.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rev\">\u003cdt>Check in \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Regular and light.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Absent.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-review-that-stopped-a-junior-from-asking\">Incident: The review that stopped a junior from asking\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A harsh pull request review made a new engineer stop sharing work until it was finished.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git log --author=maya --since=2.weeks --oneline | wc -l\ngit log --author=maya --since=6.weeks --until=2.weeks --oneline | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --author=maya --since=2.weeks --oneline | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --author=maya --since=6.weeks --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">until\u003c/span>=2.weeks --oneline | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">14\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The activity drop followed a review with sarcastic comments. The reviewer apologised, rewrote the comments with reasons and suggestions, and agreed a weekly pairing session. Commit frequency recovered within a month.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Giving feedback only at review time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Criticising the person instead of the behaviour\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Taking over the task when the mentee is slow\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Delegating with no check in\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never asking for feedback yourself\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"mentoring","text":"Mentoring"},{"id":"feedback","text":"Feedback"},{"id":"code-review-as-teaching","text":"Code review as teaching"},{"id":"receiving-feedback","text":"Receiving feedback"},{"id":"delegating","text":"Delegating"},{"id":"incident-the-review-that-stopped-a-junior-from-asking","text":"Incident: The review that stopped a junior from asking"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Code review developer guide","u":"https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/"},{"t":"Atlassian team playbook","u":"https://www.atlassian.com/team-playbook"},{"t":"Postmortem culture workbook","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/postmortem-culture/"}],"text":"Senior engineers multiply their impact by growing others. That needs skills of its own: asking good questions, giving clear feedback and delegating with the right amount of support. What you will learn What mentoring is and is not The feedback structure: observation, impact, request Reviewing code as teaching Receiving feedback Delegating for growth Mentoring A mentor helps someone reach their goals by asking questions, sharing experience and opening doors. It is not doing the work for them. Agree on goals, a regular time and what the mentee wants to practise. Let them find answers first and step in when they are stuck. Feedback State what you observed, the effect it had and what you suggest. For example: in the review you interrupted twice, so the quieter engineers stopped giving input, so please wait until others finish. Give it soon after the event, in private for criticism and in public for praise, and focus on behaviour, not character. Code review as teaching Explain the reason behind a comment and link to a guideline. Mark preferences as optional and separate them from blocking issues. Praise good solutions specifically, because people repeat what is noticed. Receiving feedback Listen to the end, ask for an example and thank the person even if you disagree. Decide later what to change. Ask peers and your manager for feedback regularly instead of waiting for a review cycle. Delegating Match the task to the person: stretching but possible. Explain the outcome you want and the limits, not each step. Agree when you will check in and let them make small mistakes. Credit them when it goes well. Mentoring and giving feedback Flow in order: 1, Goals to Observation and impact; 2, Observation and impact to Stretch task; 3, Stretch task to Check in. Outside party Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Goals Outside party What it does: Agree what to practise. If it fails or is weak: No goal. Observation and impact Step What it does: Specific feedback. If it fails or is weak: Vague. Stretch task Step What it does: Right size. If it fails or is weak: Too big. Check in Observability What it does: Regular and light. If it fails or is weak: Absent. Incident: The review that stopped a junior from asking Incident A harsh pull request review made a new engineer stop sharing work until it was finished. $ git log --author=maya --since=2.weeks --oneline | wc -l 1 $ git log --author=maya --since=6.weeks -- until =2.weeks --oneline | wc -l 14 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The activity drop followed a review with sarcastic comments. The reviewer apologised, rewrote the comments with reasons and suggestions, and agreed a weekly pairing session. Commit frequency recovered within a month. Common mistakes Giving feedback only at review time Criticising the person instead of the behaviour Taking over the task when the mentee is slow Delegating with no check in Never asking for feedback yourself"},"leadership/leading-through-incidents":{"html":"\u003cp>During an outage, technical skill matters less than clear roles and communication. A calm leader who structures the response shortens the incident and protects the team.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The incident commander role\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Communication cadence and the status update\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Decision making under uncertainty\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handoffs and fatigue\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Blameless reviews with real actions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-commander\">The commander\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The incident commander coordinates and does not debug. They assign roles: an operations lead who makes changes, a communications lead who updates stakeholders and a scribe who records the timeline. They decide when to escalate and when to roll back. If you are the best debugger, hand command to someone else.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"communication\">Communication\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Post updates on a fixed cadence, such as every thirty minutes, even if nothing changed. A good update states the impact, what is known, what is being done and when the next update comes. Keep a single channel for the incident and move side discussions elsewhere.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"decisions\">Decisions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You rarely have full information. Prefer reversible actions, such as a rollback or traffic shift, to risky fixes. State the hypothesis and the evidence that would disprove it, and time box each attempt. Ask the room for objections, then decide.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"handoffs-and-fatigue\">Handoffs and fatigue\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>After a few hours people make mistakes. Rotate roles, send tired people to rest and hand off with a written summary. A short break often saves more time than another hour of effort.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reviews\">Reviews\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A blameless review asks how the system allowed this to happen, not who caused it. Write the timeline, contributing factors and what went well. Produce a small number of actions with owners and dates and track them to completion.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"ic&gt;ops;ic&gt;comm;ops&gt;rev\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Leading through incidents\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ic\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Incident commander\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ops\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Operations lead\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"comm\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Communications lead\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rev\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Blameless review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Incident commander to Operations lead; 2, Incident commander to Communications lead; 3, Operations lead to Blameless review.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ic\">\u003cdt>Incident commander \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Coordinates.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Debugging instead.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ops\">\u003cdt>Operations lead \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Makes changes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unannounced changes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"comm\">\u003cdt>Communications lead \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Updates stakeholders.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Silence.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rev\">\u003cdt>Blameless review \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Actions and owners.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No follow up.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-an-incident-with-three-people-debugging-and-nobody-talking\">Incident: An incident with three people debugging and nobody talking\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>For two hours no update went to customers or to management.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -c &quot;STATUS UPDATE&quot; incident-4821.log\ngrep -c &quot;rolled back&quot; incident-4821.log\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;STATUS UPDATE&quot;\u003c/span> incident-4821.log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rolled back&quot;\u003c/span> incident-4821.log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Everyone was working on the fix and nobody had the communication role. A commander was named, updates went out every thirty minutes and a rollback that had been delayed was done within ten minutes of the decision.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The commander debugging as well\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No fixed update cadence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Risky fixes before a rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No handoff when tired\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Review that blames a person\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-commander","text":"The commander"},{"id":"communication","text":"Communication"},{"id":"decisions","text":"Decisions"},{"id":"handoffs-and-fatigue","text":"Handoffs and fatigue"},{"id":"reviews","text":"Reviews"},{"id":"incident-an-incident-with-three-people-debugging-and-nobody-talking","text":"Incident: An incident with three people debugging and nobody talking"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Managing incidents","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/managing-incidents/"},{"t":"Incident response workbook","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/incident-response/"},{"t":"Atlassian incident handbook","u":"https://www.atlassian.com/incident-management/handbook"}],"text":"During an outage, technical skill matters less than clear roles and communication. A calm leader who structures the response shortens the incident and protects the team. What you will learn The incident commander role Communication cadence and the status update Decision making under uncertainty Handoffs and fatigue Blameless reviews with real actions The commander The incident commander coordinates and does not debug. They assign roles: an operations lead who makes changes, a communications lead who updates stakeholders and a scribe who records the timeline. They decide when to escalate and when to roll back. If you are the best debugger, hand command to someone else. Communication Post updates on a fixed cadence, such as every thirty minutes, even if nothing changed. A good update states the impact, what is known, what is being done and when the next update comes. Keep a single channel for the incident and move side discussions elsewhere. Decisions You rarely have full information. Prefer reversible actions, such as a rollback or traffic shift, to risky fixes. State the hypothesis and the evidence that would disprove it, and time box each attempt. Ask the room for objections, then decide. Handoffs and fatigue After a few hours people make mistakes. Rotate roles, send tired people to rest and hand off with a written summary. A short break often saves more time than another hour of effort. Reviews A blameless review asks how the system allowed this to happen, not who caused it. Write the timeline, contributing factors and what went well. Produce a small number of actions with owners and dates and track them to completion. Leading through incidents Flow in order: 1, Incident commander to Operations lead; 2, Incident commander to Communications lead; 3, Operations lead to Blameless review. Step Service Outside party Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Incident commander Step What it does: Coordinates. If it fails or is weak: Debugging instead. Operations lead Service What it does: Makes changes. If it fails or is weak: Unannounced changes. Communications lead Outside party What it does: Updates stakeholders. If it fails or is weak: Silence. Blameless review Observability What it does: Actions and owners. If it fails or is weak: No follow up. Incident: An incident with three people debugging and nobody talking Incident For two hours no update went to customers or to management. $ grep -c \"STATUS UPDATE\" incident-4821.log 0 $ grep -c \"rolled back\" incident-4821.log 1 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Everyone was working on the fix and nobody had the communication role. A commander was named, updates went out every thirty minutes and a rollback that had been delayed was done within ten minutes of the decision. Common mistakes The commander debugging as well No fixed update cadence Risky fixes before a rollback No handoff when tired Review that blames a person"},"leadership/senior-staff-scope-and-career-growth":{"html":"\u003cp>Promotion is not only about doing more of the same. Expectations change from solving problems to choosing problems, and from your own output to the output of many.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The shape of senior and staff roles\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scope: from tasks to problems to strategy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing high leverage work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Visibility and sponsorship\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A personal growth plan\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"roles\">Roles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A senior engineer delivers complex work independently and improves the team. A staff engineer is trusted with ambiguous, cross team problems and sets technical direction. Titles vary by company, so read your own ladder and ask your manager for examples of strong cases.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scope\">Scope\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Early in a career you are given tasks. Later you are given problems and decide the tasks. At staff level you may define the problem itself, such as reducing deployment time across the company. Each step needs more written communication and more work through others.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"high-leverage-work\">High leverage work\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Look for work that many people reuse or that removes a recurring pain: a tool, a standard, a clear document or a hard migration. Say no to work that fills your calendar but changes nothing. Measure impact by time saved, incidents avoided or risk removed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"visibility-and-sponsorship\">Visibility and sponsorship\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write short summaries of what you did and its effect, share them with your manager and peers, and speak at internal events. A sponsor is someone senior who speaks for you when you are not in the room. Earn it with reliable results and ask directly for stretch opportunities.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"growth-plan\">Growth plan\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pick one or two skills for the next six months, such as writing design documents or leading incidents. Ask for the project that requires them, find a mentor who is good at it and review progress monthly. Keep a record of your results for performance reviews and for interviews.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"task&gt;prob;prob&gt;strat;strat&gt;imp\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Senior and staff scope and career growth\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"task\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Tasks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prob\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Problems\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"strat\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Direction\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"imp\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Impact record\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Tasks to Problems; 2, Problems to Direction; 3, Direction to Impact record.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"task\">\u003cdt>Tasks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Given to you.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Staying here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prob\">\u003cdt>Problems \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> You choose tasks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unclear goal.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"strat\">\u003cdt>Direction \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> You define problems.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No buy in.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"imp\">\u003cdt>Impact record \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Written results.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No evidence.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-engineer-who-worked-hard-and-was-not-promoted\">Incident: The engineer who worked hard and was not promoted\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A strong engineer delivered many tickets for two years and was told they were not ready for staff.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git shortlog -sn --since=2.years | head -2\ngrep -c &quot;cross-team&quot; brag-doc.md\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git shortlog -sn --since=2.years | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  612 devon\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  118 priya\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cross-team&quot;\u003c/span> brag-doc.md\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Devon was the top contributor, but all the work stayed inside one team and none of it was written up. After talking to their manager they picked a cross team reliability project, wrote a design document, kept an impact record and were promoted the following year.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Waiting to be noticed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring by volume of tickets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Work that no one else reuses\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No sponsor\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No written record of impact\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"roles","text":"Roles"},{"id":"scope","text":"Scope"},{"id":"high-leverage-work","text":"High leverage work"},{"id":"visibility-and-sponsorship","text":"Visibility and sponsorship"},{"id":"growth-plan","text":"Growth plan"},{"id":"incident-the-engineer-who-worked-hard-and-was-not-promoted","text":"Incident: The engineer who worked hard and was not promoted"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Atlassian team playbook","u":"https://www.atlassian.com/team-playbook"},{"t":"Postmortem culture","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/"},{"t":"Communication and collaboration in SRE","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/communication-and-collaboration/"}],"text":"Promotion is not only about doing more of the same. Expectations change from solving problems to choosing problems, and from your own output to the output of many. What you will learn The shape of senior and staff roles Scope: from tasks to problems to strategy Choosing high leverage work Visibility and sponsorship A personal growth plan Roles A senior engineer delivers complex work independently and improves the team. A staff engineer is trusted with ambiguous, cross team problems and sets technical direction. Titles vary by company, so read your own ladder and ask your manager for examples of strong cases. Scope Early in a career you are given tasks. Later you are given problems and decide the tasks. At staff level you may define the problem itself, such as reducing deployment time across the company. Each step needs more written communication and more work through others. High leverage work Look for work that many people reuse or that removes a recurring pain: a tool, a standard, a clear document or a hard migration. Say no to work that fills your calendar but changes nothing. Measure impact by time saved, incidents avoided or risk removed. Visibility and sponsorship Write short summaries of what you did and its effect, share them with your manager and peers, and speak at internal events. A sponsor is someone senior who speaks for you when you are not in the room. Earn it with reliable results and ask directly for stretch opportunities. Growth plan Pick one or two skills for the next six months, such as writing design documents or leading incidents. Ask for the project that requires them, find a mentor who is good at it and review progress monthly. Keep a record of your results for performance reviews and for interviews. Senior and staff scope and career growth Flow in order: 1, Tasks to Problems; 2, Problems to Direction; 3, Direction to Impact record. Outside party Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Tasks Outside party What it does: Given to you. If it fails or is weak: Staying here. Problems Step What it does: You choose tasks. If it fails or is weak: Unclear goal. Direction Step What it does: You define problems. If it fails or is weak: No buy in. Impact record Data store What it does: Written results. If it fails or is weak: No evidence. Incident: The engineer who worked hard and was not promoted Incident A strong engineer delivered many tickets for two years and was told they were not ready for staff. $ git shortlog -sn --since=2.years | head -2 612 devon 118 priya $ grep -c \"cross-team\" brag-doc.md 0 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Devon was the top contributor, but all the work stayed inside one team and none of it was written up. After talking to their manager they picked a cross team reliability project, wrote a design document, kept an impact record and were promoted the following year. Common mistakes Waiting to be noticed Measuring by volume of tickets Work that no one else reuses No sponsor No written record of impact"}});